{"id":"f99ef8ed-069b-44ef-aff2-85c4d6dcff06","object":"chat.completion","created":1793337206,"model":"deepseek-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"\n\n<｜｜DSML｜｜ calls>\n<｜｜DSML｜｜ invoke name=\"search_code\">\n<｜｜DSML｜｜ parameter name=\"query\" string=\"true\">environ</｜｜DSML｜｜ parameter>\n<｜｜DSML｜｜ parameter name=\"paths\" string=\"false\">[\"deepinfer/logger.py\", \"deepinfer/engine/stats_logger.py\"]</｜｜DSML｜｜ parameter>\n<｜｜DSML｜｜ parameter name=\"limit\" string=\"false\">20</｜｜DSML｜｜ parameter>\n</｜｜DSML｜｜ invoke>\n</｜｜DSML｜｜ calls>","reasoning_content":"Now I need to edit logger.py: extend NewLineFormatter with max_message_chars and default init reading config.\n\nLet me plan the formatter implementation:\n\n```python\n_TRUNCATION_MARKER = \"...<omitted>...\"\n\nclass NewLineFormatter(logging.Formatter):\n    def __init__(self, fmt, datefmt=None, high_precision=False, use_color=False, *, max_message_chars=None):\n        super().__init__(fmt, datefmt)\n        if max_message_chars is not None:\n            if isinstance(max_message_chars, bool) or not isinstance(max_message_chars, int) or max_message_chars < 32:\n                raise ValueError(f\"max_message_chars must be None or an integer >= 32, got {max_message_chars!r}\")\n        self.max_message_chars = max_message_chars\n        self.high_precision = high_precision\n        self.use_color = use_color\n```\n\nFor truncation: we must not mutate record.msg/args. `super().format(record)` computes record.message = record.getMessage(). Actually logging.Formatter.format sets record.message. To truncate only message body while keeping prefix, we can compute the truncated message and temporarily adjust? Requirement: prefix computed from format string with %(message)s. Best approach: compute the full formatted message via `record.getMessage()` after arg substitution; truncate; then use a temporary copy? Modifying record.message is allowed (it's set by formatter anyway, not msg/args). But other handlers run after; they call format again which recomputes record.message via getMessage() in Formatter.format. So safe.\n\nApproach: in format:\n```python\nrecord.message = record.getMessage()  # arg substitution\nif budget applies and len > budget: record.message = truncate(...)\n```\nBut exception text: logging.Formatter.format appends exc_text; that's separate from message. If we set record.message before super().format(record), super() recomputes record.message = record.getMessage() again, overwriting. So instead call super().format(record) first (gives full text with prefix and exception), then... trailing/leading slicing won't work because we need to truncate only the message body within the text.\n\nAlternative: temporarily set record.msg to the truncated message and record.args to ()? That violates \"cannot rewrite shared record's msg, args\" — even temporarily risky? Requirement says must not rewrite. So restore afterwards in a try/finally would be technically restoring, but better avoid.\n\nCleaner: compute the formatted string ourselves mimicking Formatter.format, but we can call super().format(record) after temporarily setting record.message... no.\n\nTrick: subclass approach: compute `record.message = record.getMessage()`; if needs truncation, we can build the output by calling super().format(record) — which resets record.message. Hmm, but we can then locate and replace the message substring in the formatted text? Multi-line prefix and exceptions complicate.\n\nAlternative approach: temporarily set record.msg = truncated and record.args = None... not allowed.\n\nBetter: Do the truncation on a copied record via `logging.makeLogRecord`? Requirement 5 says cannot rewrite shared original record's fields; copying into a shallow copy is fine. We could use `copy.copy(record)`? copy module is importable (\"copy\"? not in allowed list; allowed imports list is for action code imports, not the business file — business file can import anything reasonably, but instructions say \"可增加必要标准库导入\". copy is fine.)\n\nBut simpler approach: override `formatMessage`? logging.Formatter.format calls self.formatMessage(record) which returns record.message (set earlier in format()). Actually Formatter.format:\n\n```python\nrecord.message = record.getMessage()\nif self.usesTime(): record.asctime = ...\ns = self.formatMessage(record)\nif record.exc_info: ...\n```\nformatMessage: `return self._style.format(record)` -> uses record.message in the format dict. So if we override formatMessage to truncate the message for the %(message)s substitution, that works cleanly! But `record.message` remains full for other handlers (they recompute anyway).\n\nSo:\n```python\ndef formatMessage(self, record):\n    message = record.message\n    budget = self.max_message_chars\n    if budget is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True and len(message) > budget:\n        message = _truncate_message(message, budget)\n    return self._style.format(logging.makeLogRecord?) \n```\nHmm, `self._style.format(record)` uses record.__dict__['message']. To avoid mutating record.message... Actually record.message is set by our own formatter's format(); mutating it is not prohibited (prohibited: msg, args, exc_info, stack_info). But other handlers call Formatter.format which sets record.message = record.getMessage() again, so fine. Also record.message truncation could affect other custom formatters? They'd recompute. But cleanest: temporarily set record.message inside and restore in finally? Simpler: use a per-call approach with fmt replacement.\n\nActually easiest: override format entirely:\n\n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    if self._should_truncate(record):\n        truncated = _truncate_message(record.getMessage(), self.max_message_chars)\n        saved_msg, saved_args = record.msg, record.args\n        ...\n```\nNo, avoids mutation.\n\nSimplify: mutate record.message only (not in forbidden list). Implementation:\n\n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    full_message = None\n    budget = self.max_message_chars\n    if budget is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n        message = record.getMessage()\n        if len(message) > budget:\n            record.message = _truncate_message(message, budget)  # hmm getMessage recomputed in super().format\n```\nNot effective.\n\nSo override formatMessage:\n\n```python\ndef formatMessage(self, record: logging.LogRecord) -> str:\n    budget = self.max_message_chars\n    if budget is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n        message = record.message\n        if len(message) > budget:\n            saved = record.message\n            record.message = _truncate(message, budget)\n            try:\n                return super().formatMessage(record)\n            finally:\n                record.message = saved\n    return super().formatMessage(record)\n```\nThat's fine and doesn't touch msg/args.\n\nWait requirement 2: \"先完成 %s 等参数替换，再计算消息长度\" — record.message is computed by getMessage() before formatMessage, so args substitution done. Good.\n\nTruncation: budget b, marker m len 15. remaining = b - 15. head = ceil(remaining/2), tail = remaining - head. For b=32: remaining=17, head=9, tail=8. ✓\n\n`_truncate_message(message, budget)`: remaining = budget - len(_MARKER); head = (remaining + 1)//2; tail = remaining - head; return message[:head] + _MARKER + message[len(message)-tail:] if tail else message[:head]+_MARKER.\n\nAlso there's the multi-line replacement using record.message in format — since we restore record.message after, format's `record.message` will be full message; the newline handling uses prefix detection: `msg.find(record.message)` would fail (find returns -1) if the message was truncated. Prefix would be msg[:-1]? Actually `msg[: msg.find(record.message)]` = msg[:-1] — broken. Need to handle.\n\nBetter: in format, after super().format(record) we need the message used. Let's restructure: keep a truncated message, and do the multi-line prefix using the actual message rendered? Simpler: do everything in format:\n\n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    truncated = self._truncated_message(record)  # str or None\n    if truncated is not None:\n        saved = record.message\n        record.message = truncated\n        try:\n            msg = super().format(record)\n        finally:\n            record.message = saved\n        message = truncated\n    else:\n        msg = super().format(record)\n        message = record.message\n    if \"\\n\" in msg and message:\n        prefix = msg[: msg.find(message)]\n        ...\n```\nWait super().format sets record.message = record.getMessage() overriding our truncated, then formatMessage uses that... Hmm. super().format recomputes record.message. So setting before doesn't help.\n\nAlternative: override format to replicate with a temp shallow copy of the record. Use copy.copy — business code can import copy. But record copy: logging.makeLogRecord? `copy.copy(record)` copies __dict__ shallowly; setting message on copy doesn't affect original. Then super().format(copy) — but exception formatting mutates record.exc_text on the copy; fine.\n\nHowever other handlers afterwards use original record; unaffected. \n\nBut careful: `logging.Formatter.format` sets record.exc_text on the record; using a copy is safe.\n\nImplementation:\n\n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    if self._should_truncate(record):\n        record = copy.copy(record)\n        message = record.getMessage()\n        record.message = _truncate_message(message, self.max_message_chars)\n    msg = super().format(record)\n    ...\n```\nHmm but after truncating copy, super().format recomputes record.message = record.getMessage() on the copy → getMessage uses msg/args → full message. Damn.\n\nBetter: on the copy, set `record.msg = truncated; record.args = ()` — allowed since it's a copy. Then getMessage returns truncated.\n\nWait but args substitution must happen first: we call getMessage() to do substitution, then truncate, then set msg=truncated, args=() on the copy. Good.\n\nBut does the \"%(message)s\" style then format fine? yes.\n\nAlternatively simpler: don't copy; compute prefix from format string ourselves. Let's just do copy approach; import copy.\n\nActually even simpler without copy: implement truncation inside formatMessage with temporary record.message swap and restore after super().format returns. But format's newline prefix uses record.message... We can capture the used message in an instance attribute? Not thread-safe-ish but okay-ish. Hmm, thread safety: multiple threads formatting; store on record? We could compute prefix differently:\n\n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    msg = super().format(record)\n```\nLet's just do the copy approach; the copy's `msg` is set, so record.message on copy truncated and everything consistent. But wait: exception text uses record.exc_info; copy preserves.\n\nBut careful requirement \"不能改写共享原记录的 msg、args...\" — copy satisfies.\n\nAlso `logging.Formatter.format` on the copy may set exc_text etc on the copy only.\n\nImplementation details:\n\n```python\ndef _truncate_message(message: str, budget: int) -> str:\n    keep = budget - len(_TRUNCATION_MARKER)\n    head = (keep + 1) // 2\n    tail = keep - head\n    if tail <= 0:\n        return message[:head] + _TRUNCATION_MARKER\n    return message[:head] + _TRUNCATION_MARKER + message[-tail:]\n```\n\nIn format:\n```python\nbudget = self.max_message_chars\nif budget is not None and not (getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is True):\n    message = record.getMessage()\n    if len(message) > budget:\n        record = copy.copy(record)\n        record.msg = _truncate_message(message, budget)\n        record.args = ()\n```\n\nThen msg = super().format(record); newline handling uses record.message which equals truncated message (since getMessage → msg, args ()). Good.\n\nNow init reads config:\n```python\nfmt = NewLineFormatter(\n    _FORMAT,\n    datefmt=_DATE_FORMAT,\n    use_color=sys.stdout.isatty(),\n    max_message_chars=envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS,\n)\n```\nImport: `from deepinfer.environ import envs` — careful circular import? environ imports nothing from logger, fine. Actually logger might be imported before environ; environ has no logger import. But envs access triggers reading env, fine.\n\nImport as `from deepinfer import environ`? Use `from deepinfer.environ import envs`. Let me check logger.py's imports; there's no deepinfer import currently. Add `from deepinfer.environ import envs`. Hmm could add at top; but read the rest of logger.py lines 130-... to see structure? We already have first 130 and 68-130. Should be enough. Let's check the file is not using relative imports elsewhere... Let me do a quick search for \"environ\" in logger.py.\n\nAlso validation in __init__: bool not int.\n\nNow write edits.\n\nLet me first search logger.py for existing environ references."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":10054,"completion_tokens":2867,"total_tokens":12921,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":4096},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":2778},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":4096,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":5958},"system_fingerprint":"bc19ba97cbe016479903222bb1578db6"}